      "UV protection holds the warm honey color",
      "Simple wipe-on, wipe-excess application",
      "Mildew resistance for outdoor and marine teak",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Fewer tint options than Semco",
      "Color retention trails the premium marine sealers slightly",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A budget-friendly penetrating sealer that keeps teak golden without the premium price.",
    description:
      "Tip Top Teak Sealer is the value pick for homeowners and boaters who want golden teak without paying premium-brand prices. It is a genuine penetrating sealer — not an oil dressed up as one — so it soaks into the wood and protects from within, with no film to peel or flake and no sanding between coats. The UV package does the essential job of holding teak's warm honey color and slowing the inevitable march to gray, and the formula includes mildew resistance for outdoor furniture and marine use. Application follows the same easy routine as the pricier sealers: wipe it on, let it penetrate, and wipe off the excess. In our side-by-side testing it kept teak golden very capably, though its color retention trailed the top marine sealers by a small margin over the full season and it offers fewer tint choices than Semco's lineup. For the money, it punches well above its price and is the best teak sealer for a budget-conscious project.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Teak Sealer",
          item: "https://thehonestreviewers.com/best-teak-sealer-reviews/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best teak sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most people the best teak sealer is Semco Teak Sealer — it is the marine-world standard, penetrates without forming a peelable film, blocks UV graying, and comes in several tints from Natural to Classic Brown. For boat teak that takes constant salt spray, Star Brite Golden Teak Sealer is our top marine pick, and for patio and garden furniture, TotalBoat Danish Teak Sealer keeps the color natural and warm.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between teak sealer and teak oil?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Teak oil penetrates and nourishes the wood, giving a rich hand-rubbed glow, but it contains little UV protection, weathers away quickly outdoors, and can actually promote mildew on outdoor teak. Teak sealer is built to preserve the golden color long term: it carries heavy UV blockers and mildew inhibitors and lasts far longer outdoors. Use oil indoors or on protected furniture, and a sealer for outdoor and marine teak.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I let my teak turn gray or keep it golden?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Teak naturally weathers to a silver-gray patina, which is structurally harmless and preferred by some owners for its driftwood look. If you prefer the warm honey-golden color, you need a teak sealer with UV protection to block the graying. Both are valid — graying requires no upkeep, while keeping teak golden requires cleaning and resealing roughly once a year.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do you apply teak sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Clean the teak with a two-part teak cleaner and brightener to strip old finish, gray surface, and mildew, then rinse and let it dry completely for a day or two. Apply the sealer in thin coats with a brush, cloth, or sprayer, let it penetrate, then wipe off any excess before it gets tacky. Most sealers take two or three thin coats; never flood a thick coat on, as the excess stays sticky.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should you reseal teak?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most teak sealers last about a year on outdoor furniture and need touch-ups a few times a season on sun-and-salt-exposed marine teak. Reseal when the golden color starts to dull or fade toward gray, or when water stops beading. A light cleaning and a maintenance coat is all that is needed — you rarely have to strip back to bare wood once a good sealing routine is established.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my teak turning black or developing mildew?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Black spotting on teak is mildew, and it is most common on oiled teak because teak oil can feed mold and mildew in damp or humid conditions. Switch from an oil to a penetrating teak sealer with mildew inhibitors, clean the existing mildew off with a teak cleaner first, and make sure the wood dries fully before sealing. A proper sealer resists the black spotting that oils encourage.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-03-20",
      dateModified: "2026-03-20",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
