        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Seal -->
        <div id="why-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why You Should Seal Your Teak (And the Gray-vs-Golden Choice)
          </h2>
          <p>
            Teak is prized because it is naturally rich in oils and silica that make it astonishingly resistant to rot, insects, and water — which is why it has been the wood of choice for boat decks and high-end patio furniture for centuries. But that durability does not extend to its color. Left exposed to sun and weather, teak's warm golden-honey tone breaks down at the surface and the wood weathers to a soft silver-gray patina. That graying is purely cosmetic and structurally harmless, and plenty of owners genuinely prefer the driftwood look.
          </p>
          <p>
            The other camp wants to keep that warm golden glow — and that is where a <strong>teak sealer</strong> comes in. A quality sealer penetrates the wood and carries UV blockers that stop the graying, plus mildew inhibitors that prevent the black spotting common on outdoor and marine teak. Unlike a varnish, a good sealer forms no thick film to peel or flake. The choice is yours: let it gray and do nothing, or keep it golden and plan on a clean-and-reseal roughly once a year. There is no wrong answer — but if you want golden teak, a sealer is the only practical way to get it.
          </p>

