          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Apply Teak Sealer: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>
          <p>
            Sealing teak is straightforward, but skipping the prep is what separates a finish that lasts a year from one that fails in a month. Here's the process that gives professional results:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clean With a Teak Cleaner</h3>
          <p>
            Start with a dedicated <strong>teak cleaner</strong> to strip away dirt, old oil or sealer, surface grime, and any mildew. Work it in with a soft brush, scrubbing <em>across</em> the grain so you don't gouge soft fibers, then rinse thoroughly. Clean wood is non-negotiable — sealer bonds to bare teak, not to a layer of weathered oil and dirt.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Brighten the Wood</h3>
          <p>
            Most cleaners come as a two-part system: a cleaner followed by a <strong>brightener</strong> (an acid wash) that neutralizes the cleaner and restores the warm tone to gray, weathered teak. This step evens out the color and gives the sealer a clean, bright surface to lock in. Rinse well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Let It Dry Completely</h3>
          <p>
            This is the step people skip and regret. The teak must be <strong>bone dry</strong> — typically 24-48 hours of dry weather after cleaning. Sealing damp wood traps moisture, ruins adhesion, and invites mildew. Make sure the grain has dried fully before you open the can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Apply Thin Coats</h3>
          <p>
            Apply the sealer in <strong>thin, even coats</strong> with a brush, cloth, or sprayer, working with the grain in manageable sections. Most teak sealers want two or three thin coats rather than one heavy one. Penetrating sealers are water-thin, so mask off any surrounding surfaces because they drip and splatter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Wipe Off the Excess</h3>
          <p>
            After each coat penetrates, <strong>wipe off any excess</strong> with a clean cloth before it gets tacky. This is the secret to a finish that looks natural instead of sticky — a sealer is meant to soak in, not build up on the surface. Let it cure per the label before exposing the teak to water or heavy use.
          </p>
        </div>

