        <!-- SECTION: Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            Common Teak Sealing Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>
          <p>
            Almost every teak finish that fails early fails for the same handful of reasons — and none of them have anything to do with the brand of sealer. After watching dozens of test panels and reader projects go wrong, we keep seeing the same avoidable errors. Get these right and even a budget sealer will outlast a premium product applied carelessly.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Weathered, Gray Teak Without Cleaning and Brightening First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the single most common mistake, and it dooms the finish before you even open the can. Gray teak is not just discolored — its surface is a layer of dead, oxidized wood fibers, embedded dirt, and often a film of old oil. Sealer applied over that bonds to the loose gray layer, not to sound wood, so it lifts and flakes within weeks and the gray bleeds right back through. You must scrub with a two-part teak cleaner to strip the weathered surface, then follow with the brightener to neutralize the cleaner and pull the warm honey tone back to the surface. Only then is the wood ready to take sealer. Skipping this step is why people complain that "teak sealer doesn't work" — the sealer was fine; the prep was not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Sealer to Damp Wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                After cleaning and rinsing, teak looks dry on the surface long before the grain underneath has actually dried out. Sealing damp wood is a recipe for trouble: a penetrating sealer cannot soak into pores that are already full of water, so it sits on top, stays blotchy, and never properly locks in the color. Worse, you can seal moisture into the wood, where it feeds mildew and causes black spotting from the inside out. Give freshly cleaned teak a full 24 to 48 hours of dry weather before you seal, and never seal first thing in the morning when dew or overnight humidity has soaked back in. If you can, check the forecast and plan your sealing day around a dry, mild stretch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flooding On Coats That Are Too Thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Penetrating teak sealers are water-thin by design — they are meant to soak into the grain, not build a film on the surface. The instinct to "put more on for more protection" backfires badly here. Any sealer the wood cannot absorb just sits on top, where it dries to a sticky, tacky, uneven sheen that attracts dust and feels gummy underfoot or on your hands. The right technique is the opposite of generous: apply thin, even coats, let each one penetrate, and then wipe off every bit of excess with a clean cloth before it can get tacky. Two or three thin coats will always outperform one heavy one, and they cure faster and look far more natural.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Teak Oil Outdoors Where It Grows Mildew</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Teak oil is wonderful on indoor furniture, but reaching for it on a patio set or a boat deck is a mistake that shows up as black spotting within a season. Oil contains little real UV protection, so it weathers off outdoors in weeks to a couple of months — but the bigger problem is that the oil itself becomes food for mold and mildew in any damp or humid environment. The result is the grimy black blotching that so many people mistake for the wood rotting. If your teak lives outdoors or near salt water, skip the oil and use a penetrating sealer with built-in mildew inhibitors. Save the oil for protected indoor pieces where mildew is not a threat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Maintenance Schedule Slip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A teak sealer is not a one-and-done product, and treating it like one is how golden teak quietly slides back to gray. UV protection wears down gradually, and the first sign is the color dulling and losing its warmth — well before the wood looks bad. If you wait until the teak has fully grayed again, you are back to the full strip-clean-brighten cycle. Stay ahead of it instead: a light cleaning and a fresh maintenance coat roughly once a year on furniture, or a few quick touch-ups across the season on sun-and-salt-blasted marine teak, keeps the color locked in with a fraction of the effort. Catching the fade early is the difference between a five-minute wipe-on coat and a weekend of scrubbing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION: How to Choose -->
        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            How to Choose the Right Teak Sealer
          </h2>
          <p>
            With a dozen products promising "golden teak forever," it helps to narrow the field by the factors that actually change the result. The right choice depends mostly on where your teak lives and what look you are after — not on price alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Start with oil versus sealer.</strong> This is the fork in the road that decides everything else. If your teak is outdoors or on the water, you almost always want a true penetrating <em>sealer</em>, because it carries the heavy UV blockers and mildew inhibitors that keep color from graying and surfaces from spotting black. A teak <em>oil</em> nourishes the grain and gives a gorgeous hand-rubbed glow, but it offers little lasting UV defense and can feed mildew outdoors. Reserve oils for indoor furniture and protected pieces, or use one purely as a color-restoring base coat before sealing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Match the product to marine versus furniture use.</strong> Boat teak takes a beating that patio furniture never sees — constant salt spray, salt water, and relentless reflected sun. Dedicated marine sealers like Star Brite Golden Teak Sealer pack tropical-grade UV inhibitors and salt resistance built for that punishment, and they reapply right over the previous coat for easy in-season touch-ups. Furniture-focused sealers like TotalBoat Danish Teak Sealer lean toward natural-looking color and low build for chairs, benches, and tables. Semco straddles both worlds and is the reason it is the marine standard as well as a favorite for furniture.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Then choose your tint and confirm the UV rating.</strong> Color is where sealers shine over plain oils: Semco alone offers five tints, from a near-neutral Natural through Honeytone and Goldtone to a deep Classic Brown, plus a Cleartone UV sealer that simply preserves whatever color the wood currently is. Decide whether you want a freshly-sanded blond, the classic yacht honey-gold, or a deeper brown, and always test the tint on a hidden spot first, because different boards take color differently. Above all, confirm the product actually advertises real UV protection — that is the single feature doing the heavy lifting of keeping teak golden rather than gray.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION: How Long It Lasts -->
        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            How Long Teak Sealer Lasts and When to Reapply
          </h2>
          <p>
            There is no single answer to how long teak sealer lasts, because the wear rate is driven almost entirely by exposure. On <strong>outdoor furniture</strong> that sees normal sun and weather, a good penetrating sealer typically holds its color for about a year before it needs a maintenance coat. A teak deck or furniture set kept in partial shade or under a cover can stretch well beyond that, while pieces baking in full, all-day sun will fade noticeably sooner. The wood itself does not need the protection — teak is rot-resistant on its own — so what you are really tracking is how long the UV package keeps the golden color from washing out.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Boat decks and marine teak</strong> are the harshest case by a wide margin. Direct overhead sun, sun reflecting up off the water, and constant salt spray break a sealer down far faster than the same product would wear on a shaded patio. Most boat owners plan on touch-up coats a few times across a season rather than once a year, and they accept that frequent, light maintenance is simply part of keeping a bright teak deck looking its best. The good news is that marine sealers are built for exactly this — they reapply right over the previous coat with no sanding, so a touch-up is a quick wipe-on job.
          </p>
          <p>
            The smart way to manage reapplication is to watch the teak, not the calendar. Reseal when the warm tone starts to dull toward gray or when water stops beading on the surface — both are early signs the UV protection is wearing thin. Because a penetrating sealer leaves no film to strip or sand, a maintenance coat goes straight over the existing finish after a quick cleaning. Stay ahead of the fade with these light coats and you essentially never have to take the wood back to bare again, which is the whole payoff of a consistent sealing routine.
          </p>
